%U A399503 1350,1211,1561,1531,1793,1760,2047,1933,2324,2284,2625,2581,2951,2807,3303,3251,3682,3626,4089,3798,4525,4460,4991,4921,5488,5273
%N A399503 The number of distinct regions in a regular n-gon with all diagonals, up to dihedral automorphisms of the n-gon.
%C A399503 Equivalently, the number of equivalence classes of an equivalence relation on the regions in a regular n-gon with all diagonals, where two regions are equivalent iff they can be mapped to each other by dihedral rotations and reflections of the n-gon.
%C A399503 Equivalently, in a regular n-gon with all diagonals, the maximum number of colors that can be used to color the regions while preserving all dihedral symmetries.
%C A399503 Thanks to Arthur O'Dwyer and Bradley Klee for their help naming this sequence.
%C A399503 A brief explanation of the result follows. Let C_0(n), C_1(n), and C_2(n) be the number of equivalence classes with 1, n, and 2*n members, respectively. Let R(n) be the number of regions. Then a(n) = C_0(n) + C_1(n) + C_2(n) and R(n) = 1*C_0(n) + n*C_1(n) + 2*n*C_2(n). Eliminating C_2 gives a(n) = C_0 + (R(n) - C_0(n) + n*C_1(n)) / (2*n).
%C A399503 R(n) is A007678. C_0(n) is 1 for odd n and 0 for even n, which is A000035. C_1(n) is found as follows. Choose an axis of symmetry of the n-gon: between edge midpoints for even n or edge midpoint to vertex for odd n. Members of the C_1(n) equivalence classes lie along such an axis. Let J(n) be the number of distinct intersections of diagonals with the axis. (The sides are considered diagonals.)  Then C_1(n) = J(n) - 2 for odd n and C_1(n) = (J(n) - 1)/2 for even n.
%C A399503 The intersections are of degrees 1, 2, 3, 4, and 6. (The center (even n) or axis vertex (odd n) are counted separately, without regard to degree, as the leading 1 in J(n) below.) Intersections of degrees 1 consist of a single diagonal perpendicular to the axis, while those of degree 2 consist of a symmetric pair of diagonals. Both are easily counted assuming there are no intersections of degree larger than 2. When there are such intersections the number of distinct intersections is reduced. A set of ~n/2 intersections of degree 4 exists when n is a multiple of 6. Additionally, five fixed sets of intersections with degrees 3, 4, or 6 exist and occur periodically in n. Together these completely describe J(n): J(n) = 1 + (n^2-1)/8 * [(n,4)=1] + (n^2-4)/8 * [(n,4)=2] + (n^2)/8 * [(n,4)=4] + (-n/2+3) * [(n,24)=6] + (-n/2+4) * [(n,24)=12] + (-n/2) * [(n,24)=24] + (-2) * [(n,24)=12] + (-8) * [(n,60)=30] + (-8) * [(n,60)=30] + (-12) * [(n,84)=42] + (-12) * [(n,120)=60], where (a,b) is gcd(a,b) and [] is the Iverson bracket. The resulting formula for a(n), translated into the style of Poonen and Rubenstein, appears in the Formula section. It has been proved that for all n the formula correctly accounts for all observed effects, and checked over the range 3<=n<=5000. However, additional as-yet-unobserved effects might exist beyond that range.
%H A399503 Christopher Scussel, <a href="/A399503/b399503.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 3..5000</a>
%H A399503 Christopher Scussel, <a href="/A399503/a399503_1.pdf">Derivation of A399503</a>
%H A399503 Christopher Scussel, <a href="/A399503/a399503_2.pdf">Illustrations for n=3..10</a>
%F A399503 It appears that for all n: a(n) = (n^3 - 3*n^2 + 23*n - 21 + (-4*n^2 + 21*n - 47) * delta_2(n) - 12 * delta_4(n) + (-112*n + 656) * delta_6(n)) / 48 + 12 * delta_12(n) + 16 * delta_18(n) + 9 * delta_24(n) - 22 * delta_30(n) - 28 * delta_42(n) - 94 * delta_60(n) - 36 * delta_84(n) - 24 * delta_90(n) - 36 * delta_120(n) - 24 * delta_210(n), where delta_k(n)=1 if k divides n, and zero otherwise.
%e A399503 See the Illustrations link. Also, a(n)=A187781(n) for n<12 but differs at n=12 due to congruent regions which are not related by dihedral rotations and reflections in A187781(12).
%Y A399503 Cf. A000035, A007678, A187781.
